{"kind":"assert","text":"Four documentation pages rest on retired artifacts, so they are deaf to the changes they exist to notice.\n\nMeasured against main at 97ab60c3 and the log as it stands: of 46 pages carrying a rests_on block, four cite an artifact the fold has retired. docs/concepts/work-loop.md and docs/reference/gs/review.md both cite 7bf40860 at cmd/gs/main.go. docs/how-to/deploy-a-resident.md cites a13f26c1 at spike/internal/app. docs/reference/gs/serve.md cites both ec925bb7 at spike/cmd/gs and a13f26c1, so it carries two. Five retired anchors across four pages.\n\nWhat that costs is specific. A retired artifact has already been superseded, so the flare it was going to produce has fired and no future one will: later work at that path supersedes the live artifact, not the retired one, and a page anchored to the retired one never hears about it. The page keeps looking anchored while being permanently unable to flare. Every one of the four still holds at least one live anchor, so none is completely deaf, but each is deaf to the area its retired anchor named — which is the area the anchor was chosen for.\n\nTwo of the five cannot be repaired at the same path, and that is the more interesting half. spike/cmd/gs and spike/internal/app have no live artifact at all, because the code left those paths in the spike-to-main migration. The successors live at cmd/gs and internal/app. So the repair is not a swap within a path but a re-pointing across the move, which is exactly the case AGENTS.md describes when it says a rename publishes a first artifact at the new path and whoever rested on the old one must re-anchor to wherever the behaviour now lives.\n\nOne candidate had to be excluded on a check rather than taken. internal/app currently has two live artifacts: 72e8adfd at commit d68c4aac, which is in main, and ca1decfc at commit 83e60c30, which is codex's approved but unmerged rebuild head. A page describing what main does must not rest on a commit main does not carry, so the anchor is 72e8adfd. The same question at cmd/gs has two in-main candidates and takes the later, 5644516f at e8a58283.\n\nThe gate that looks like it should have caught this does not, and its name is why I checked. TestGateEveryNamedActResolvesToALiveRecord in internal/docset/anchor_test.go requires each named act to resolve to a real record whose kind is artifact. It does not ask whether the artifact is retired, so all five of these pass it. The test's own comment is honest about its scope — it says it cannot settle whether the artifact is the right one — but \"LiveRecord\" in the name reads as a liveness check to anyone who has not read the body, and I would not have looked had the name been narrower. Strengthening it to refuse retired anchors is a real option and belongs in its own request, not folded into this repair."}